## Getting Started

### Prerequisites
- [Flutter SDK](https://docs.flutter.dev/get-started/install) (stable channel)
- Android Studio or VS Code with the Flutter and Dart plugins
- An Android phone (USB debugging on) or an Android emulator
- A Google account for Firebase
- Git

### Clone and install

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-org>/automate.git
cd automate
flutter pub get
flutter doctor        # make sure everything is green
```

### Run

```bash
flutter run
```

> The app needs a Firebase project to run. Complete the next section first.

---

## Firebase Setup

1. Create a project in the [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/).
2. Enable **Authentication → Sign-in method → Email/Password**.
3. Create a **Cloud Firestore** database.
4. Enable **Firebase Storage**. *(Confirm early that Storage is available on your plan; images are compressed before upload to stay within free limits.)*
5. Connect the app to your project using the FlutterFire CLI:

   ```bash
   dart pub global activate flutterfire_cli
   flutterfire configure
   ```

   This generates `lib/firebase_options.dart`. Do **not** commit private keys or secrets.
6. Deploy the security rules:

   ```bash
   firebase deploy --only firestore:rules,storage
   ```

7. **Create the first admin:** register a normal account in the app, then in the Firestore console open `users/<uid>` and set `role` to `admin`.

### Using the Firebase Emulator (recommended for development and rule tests)

```bash
firebase emulators:start
```

---

## Data Model

Main Firestore collections:

| Collection | Purpose |
|------------|---------|
| `users` | Profile and role (`customer`, `shop`, `admin`) |
| `vehicles` | Customer vehicles (brand, model, type, registration, mileage) |
| `centres` | Service centres (location, hours, bank details, status, average rating) |
| `serviceItems` | Master list of service categories |
| `centreServices` | A centre's services with price per vehicle type and oil-brand options |
| `timeSlots` | Date, start/end time, capacity and booked count |
| `bookings` | Customer, centre, vehicle, slot, items, total price, status |
| `paymentSlips` | Slip image URL and verification status |
| `reviews` | Rating (1–5) and comment, one per completed booking |
| `serviceRecords` | Vehicle service history created from completed bookings |
| `notifications` | In-app notifications and reminder records |

An ER diagram and UML diagrams (use case, class, sequence, state) are provided in `docs/` (`AutoMate_UML.drawio`).

---

## Booking Status Flow

```
Pending ──► Confirmed ──► In Progress ──► Completed
   │            │
   └────────────┴──► Cancelled
```

- **Pending:** booking created and slot reserved; awaiting payment slip and confirmation
- **Confirmed:** payment verified / shop confirmed; reminders scheduled
- **In Progress:** vehicle received and service started
- **Completed:** service finished; service record created and review enabled
- **Cancelled:** cancelled by customer or rejected by shop; slot released

---

## Testing

```bash
flutter analyze
flutter test                        # unit and widget tests
flutter test integration_test       # integration tests (device/emulator)
```

Planned test areas: authentication and roles, distance calculation, nearby search and filters, price comparison, slot availability, **simultaneous booking of the last slot**, payment-slip flow, reminders, service history, reviews, offline behaviour, and Firestore/Storage security rules (Firebase Emulator).

Usability will be evaluated with the System Usability Scale (target SUS ≥ 68).

---

## Scope

**In scope (first version):** Android app for three roles; email/password auth; nearby search; shop-specific catalogues and price comparison; slot-based booking with live availability; bank-slip upload with manual verification; booking tracking, local reminders and in-app notifications; ratings, reviews and service history; admin screens with basic reports.

**Out of scope:** iOS and web versions, online payment gateways, automatic slip (OCR) verification, embedded routing maps and live tracking, in-app chat, pick-up and delivery, server-side push notifications at scale (stretch goal), AI-based fault prediction.

